			<!-- Section 1: The Exact Song Math Formula -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; text-align: center;">
					1. The Exact Math Behind Wedding Playlist Sizing
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
					The average commercial radio song duration across Pop, R&B, Rock, and Soul is <strong>3 minutes and 30 seconds (3.5 minutes)</strong>. In a continuous, non-stop hour of music, you will hear approximately <strong>17 songs</strong>.
				</p>
				<p class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
					However, during the peak dance floor party, professional wedding DJs rarely let songs play from the opening beat to the final 45-second acoustic fade-out. Instead, DJs use <em>"Quick-Mix" transitions</em>—playing the first verse, pre-chorus, and iconic chorus before seamlessly beatmatching into the next track at the 2:15 to 2:45 mark. This accelerates dancing pace to <strong>20–24 songs per hour</strong>.
				</p>

				<!-- Sizing Matrix Table -->
				<div style="overflow-x: auto; background: #fff; border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
